To add icons to each section header automatically, add this JavaScript:

```javascript
// Add icons to section headers (add this to your initApp function or after DOM loads)
function enhanceSectionHeaders() {
    const sectionIcons = {
        'dashboard': 'bi-speedometer2',
        'projects': 'bi-folder',
        'assessments': 'bi-clipboard-check',
        'species': 'bi-tree',
        'maintenance': 'bi-tools',
        'charts': 'bi-bar-chart',
        'data': 'bi-database',
        'weather': 'bi-cloud-sun'
    };
    
    document.querySelectorAll('.content-section').forEach(section => {
        const header = section.querySelector('.section-header');
        if (header && !header.querySelector('h2 i')) {
            const h2 = header.querySelector('h2');
            const sectionId = section.id;
            const icon = sectionIcons[sectionId];
            
            if (h2 && icon) {
                // Add icon to the beginning of h2
                const iconElement = document.createElement('i');
                iconElement.className = `${icon} me-2`;
                iconElement.style.color = 'var(--accent)';
                h2.insertBefore(iconElement, h2.firstChild);
                
                // Add the with-icon class if using that version
                header.classList.add('with-icon');
            }
        }
    });
}

// Call this after loading content sections
// Add to your initApp function or call it when sections become active
```

The first CSS option (with the colored left border) is probably the best balance - it adds visual separation from the sidebar, creates a clear hierarchy, and looks professional without being too flashy. The 4px colored border on the left creates a subtle but effective visual gap that makes the header feel separate from the sidebar.
